sunny line\n\nWe examine every possible non\u2011sunny line and show that the remaining points cannot be covered by two sunny lines.\n\n| Type of non\u2011sunny line | Equation | Points of \\(S\\) on it | Remaining points | Possibility of covering the remainder with two sunny lines |\n|------------------------|----------|------------------------|------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|\n| Vertical               | \\(x=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Only three points left, but any sunny line can contain at most two of them; the three cannot be split into two sunny lines (any pair among them is either horizontal or vertical). |\n| Vertical               | \\(x=2\\)  | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\)       | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | The only admissible sunny pair among the four is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)). The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) would then each need a separate sunny line, requiring three sunny lines \u2013 impossible. |\n| Vertical               | \\(x=3\\)  | \\((3,1)\\)             | five points left \u2013 a non\u2011sunny line can cover at most three points, so two sunny lines (max four points) cannot cover five. |\n| Horizontal             | \\(y=1\\)  | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) | No two of these three have an allowed slope (they are vertical or have slope \\(-1\\)). Hence at least three sunny lines would be needed. |\n| Horizontal             | \\(y=2\\)  | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\)       | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | The only admissible sunny pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)). The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) remain and cannot be joined by a sunny line (they are horizontal). |\n| Horizontal             | \\(y=3\\)  | \\((1,3)\\)             | five points left \u2013 impossible as in the \\(x=3\\) case. |\n| Diagonal (\\(x+y=c\\))   | \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\)             | five points left \u2013 impossible. |\n| Diagonal (\\(x+y=c\