            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le 4\\}\n   =\\{(1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\}.\n\\]\n\nWe must cover all points of \\(S\\) with three distinct lines, exactly two of which are *sunny* (i.e. not parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis, the \\(y\\)-axis, nor to the line \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  How many points can a single line contain?\n\n*Non‑sunny lines.*  \nA line parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis has equation \\(y=c\\); among the points of \\(S\\) such a line can contain at most the three points with that \\(y\\)-coordinate (e.g. \\(y=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\)).  \nA line parallel to the \\(y\\)-axis has equation \\(x=c\\); similarly it can contain at most three points (e.g. \\(x=1\\) contains \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\)).  \nA line parallel to \\(x+y=0\\) has equation \\(x+y=c\\); for \\(c\\in\\{2,3,4\\}\\) it contains respectively \\(1,2,3\\) points of \\(S\\).  \nHence any non‑sunny line contains **at most three** points of \\(S\\).\n\n*Sunny lines.*  \nA sunny line is not horizontal, vertical, nor of slope \\(-1\\).  \nConsider any three distinct points of \\(S\\). The slopes between each pair are listed below:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{c|c}\n\\text{pair} & \\text{slope}\\\\ \\hline\n(1,1)-(1,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,1)-(2,2) & 1\\\\\n(1,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(1,3) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,1) & -1\\\\\n(1,2)-(2,2) & 0\\\\\n(1,2)-(3,1) & -\\tfrac12\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,1) & -2\\\\\n(1,3)-(2,2) & -1\\\\\n(1,3)-(3,1) & -1\\\\\n(2,1)-(2,2) & \\text{vertical}\\\\\n(2,1)-(3,1) & 0\\\\\n(2,2)-(3,1) & -1\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\nThe only slopes that appear in **three or more** pairs are vertical, horizontal (slope \\(0\\)), and \\(-1\\). Consequently any three collinear points of \\(S\\) must be either vertical, horizontal, or lie on a line of slope \\(-1\\). Since a sunny line cannot have any of these three slopes, a sunny line can contain **at most two** points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Consequences for a configuration with two sunny lines\n\nLet the three required lines be \\(L_{1},L_{2},L_{3}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.  \nBecause each sunny line can cover at most two points of \\(S\\), the two sunny lines together cover at most four points.  \nTherefore the remaining (non‑sunny) line must cover the other two or three points of \\(S\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Exhaustion of the possible non‑sunny line\n\nThe only non‑sunny lines that intersect \\(S\\) are  \n\n* vertical: \\(x=1,\\;x=2,\\;x=3\\);  \n* horizontal: \\(y=1,\\;y=2,\\;y=3\\);  \n* diagonal (slope \\(-1\\)): \\(x+y=2,\\;x+y=3,\\;x+y=4\\).\n\nFor each of these nine possibilities we list the points of \\(S\\) left uncovered and show that they cannot be covered by two sunny lines (each sunny line can contain at most two points, and any admissible pair must have a slope different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\)).  \n\n| Non‑sunny line | Points of \\(S\\) on it | Remaining points | Reason they cannot be covered by two sunny lines |\n|----------------|-----------------------|------------------|---------------------------------------------------|\n| \\(x=1\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3)\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | Every pair among the three remaining points is vertical, horizontal or slope \\(-1\\); thus no sunny line can contain two of them. |\n| \\(x=2\\) | \\((2,1),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1)\\) | The only admissible pair is \\((1,2),(3,1)\\) (slope \\(-\\tfrac12\\)). The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,3)\\) would each need a separate sunny line, requiring three sunny lines. |\n| \\(x=3\\) | \\((3,1)\\) | five points left – impossible because two sunny lines can cover at most four points. |\n| \\(y=1\\) | \\((1,1),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,2),(1,3),(2,2)\\) | All three pairs are forbidden slopes (vertical, horizontal, or \\(-1\\)). |\n| \\(y=2\\) | \\((1,2),(2,2)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,1),(3,1)\\) | The only admissible pair is \\((1,3),(2,1)\\) (slope \\(-2\\)). The points \\((1,1)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would each need a separate sunny line. |\n| \\(y=3\\) | \\((1,3)\\) | five points left – impossible. |\n| \\(x+y=2\\) | \\((1,1)\\) | five points left – impossible. |\n| \\(x+y=3\\) | \\((1,2),(2,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | The only admissible pair is \\((1,1),(2,2)\\) (slope \\(1\\)). The points \\((1,3)\\) and \\((3,1)\\) would each need a separate sunny line. |\n| \\(x+y=4\\) | \\((1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\) | \\((1,1),(1,2),(2,1)\\) | No pair among the three remaining points has an allowed slope; all are vertical, horizontal or \\(-1\\). |\n\nIn every possible case a contradiction is reached: the points left uncovered cannot be partitioned into two sunny lines (each line would have to contain a pair with a forbidden slope, or we would need more than two sunny lines).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Conclusion  \n\nSince every admissible choice for the non‑sunny line leads to an impossibility, **no configuration of three distinct lines with exactly two sunny lines can cover all points of \\(S\\)**.  \n