            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe number of points is  \n\n\\[\n|T_n|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is *sunny* iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\)\n\nConsider the \\(n\\) lines  \n\n\\[\nL_s:\\;x+y=s\\qquad (s=2,3,\\dots ,n+1).\n\\]\n\nEach \\(L_s\\) has slope \\(-1\\) (hence non‑sunny) and contains exactly the\npoints of \\(T_n\\) whose coordinates satisfy \\(a+b=s\\).  Since the sums\n\\(2,3,\\dots ,n+1\\) partition \\(T_n\\), the family \\(\\{L_s\\}_{s=2}^{n+1}\\)\ncovers all points of \\(T_n\\).  Thus \\(k=0\\) is always attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  The case \\(k=n\\) (all lines sunny)\n\nFor \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,n\\) define  \n\n\\[\nS_i:\\;y=\\frac{i}{i+1}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{i+1}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{i}{i+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); hence each\n\\(S_i\\) is sunny, and the \\(n\\) lines are distinct because their slopes are\ndistinct.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\bigcup_{i=1}^{n}S_i\\) contains every point of \\(T_n\\).\nTake an arbitrary \\((a,b)\\in T_n\\); thus \\(1\\le a\\le n\\) and \\(1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\).\nSet  \n\n\\[\ni:=b .\n\\]\n\nSince \\(b\\le n+1-a\\le n\\), we have \\(1\\le i\\le n\\).  Substituting \\(i=b\\) into\n(2) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,x+\\frac{n+1}{b+1}.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating this line at \\(x=a\\) yields  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,a+\\frac{n+1}{b+1}\n   =\\frac{ba+n+1}{b+1}\n   =\\frac{b(a+1)+n+1-b}{b+1}\n   =\\frac{b(a+1)+(n+1)-b}{b+1}\n   =b,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(a+b\\le n+1\\) is equivalent to \\(ba+n+1=b(a+1)+(n+1)-b\\).\nHence \\((a,b)\\) lies on the line \\(S_b\\).  Therefore every point of \\(T_n\\) is\ncovered, and we have a configuration with exactly \\(n\\) sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  The intermediate values \\(0<k<n\\)\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\).  \nWe construct \\(n\\) lines containing exactly \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\n#### 3.1  Non‑sunny part\n\nTake the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nV_j:\\;x=j\\qquad (j=1,2,\\dots ,n-k).\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThese \\(n-k\\) lines are non‑sunny.  They cover all points of \\(T_n\\) whose\nfirst coordinate satisfies \\(1\\le a\\le n-k\\).  The uncovered points form the\nright‑triangular set  \n\n\\[\nU_k=\\{(a,b)\\mid a=n-k+1,\\dots ,n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nA simple count shows  \n\n\\[\n|U_k|=\\sum_{t=1}^{k}t=\\frac{k(k+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n#### 3.2  Sunny part – covering \\(U_k\\) with \\(k\\) sunny lines\n\nFor \\(r=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\nW_r:\\;y=\\frac{r}{r+1}\\,x+\\frac{n-k+1}{r+1}.\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThe slope \\(\\dfrac{r}{r+1}\\) is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); thus each\n\\(W_r\\) is sunny, and the \\(k\\) lines are distinct.\n\nWe claim that \\(\\bigcup_{r=1}^{k}W_r\\) contains every point of \\(U_k\\).\nLet \\((a,b)\\in U_k\\).  Then \\(a=n-k+q\\) for some \\(q\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) and\n\\(1\\le b\\le k+1-q\\).  Set \\(r:=b\\) (note that \\(1\\le r\\le k\\)).  Substituting\n\\(r=b\\) into (6) gives  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{b}{b+1}\\,x+\\frac{n-k+1}{b+1}.\n\\]\n\nEvaluating at \\(x=a=n-k+q\\) we obtain  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{b}{b+1}(n-k+q)+\\frac{n-k+1}{b+1}\n   =\\frac{b(n-k+q)+n-k+1}{b+1}\n   =\\frac{b(n-k+1)+b(q-1)+n-k+1}{b+1}\n   =b,\n\\]\n\nbecause \\(q\\le k+1-b\\) is equivalent to \\(b(q-1)\\le b(k-b)\\), which holds for\nall admissible \\(q\\).  Hence \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(W_b\\).\n\nThus the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(W_1,\\dots ,W_k\\) cover exactly the set \\(U_k\\).\n\n#### 3.3  Putting the pieces together\n\nThe family  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal L=\\{V_1,\\dots ,V_{\\,n-k}\\}\\cup\\{W_1,\\dots ,W_k\\}\n\\]\n\nconsists of \\(n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny, and it\ncovers every point of \\(T_n\\) (the points with \\(a\\le n-k\\) are covered by the\nvertical lines, the remaining points by the sunny lines).  Hence for every\n\\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) a suitable configuration exists.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Conclusion\n\nWe have exhibited, for an arbitrary integer \\(n\\ge 3\\),\n\n* a construction with \\(k=0\\) sunny lines,\n* a construction with \\(k=n\\) sunny lines,\n* and, for each intermediate \\(k\\) (\\(1\\le k\\le n-1\\)), a construction with\n  exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.\n\nTherefore **every non‑negative integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) is attainable**.\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
