            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z_{>0}^2\\mid a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge3 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and\n\\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  A necessary condition\n\nIf a line is not sunny it is either vertical, horizontal or has slope\n\\(-1\\).  Consequently a sunny line can contain **at most one** point from\nany vertical line, at most one point from any horizontal line and at most\none point from any line of slope \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the three points  \n\n\\[\nP_1=(m+1,1),\\;P_2=(m+1,2),\\;P_3=(m+2,1),\\qquad\nm=n-2 .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nThey belong to \\(T_n\\) (they satisfy \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  Any two of them are\naligned vertically, horizontally or with slope \\(-1\\); therefore a sunny\nline can contain **at most one** of the three points.  Hence at least three\nsunny lines are needed to cover them.  This shows that a configuration with\nexactly two sunny lines cannot exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\neq2}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  Constructions for the remaining values of \\(k\\)\n\nWrite \\(m=n-k\\).  The points of \\(T_n\\) with first coordinate \\(\\le m\\) are\ncovered by the vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nAll these lines are non‑sunny.  The points that are still uncovered form the\nright‑angled triangle  \n\n\\[\n\\Delta_{k}:=\\{(m+i,j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\}.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nIts cardinality is \\(|\\Delta_{k}|=k(k+1)/2\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.1  The cases \\(k=0\\) and \\(k=1\\)\n\n* **\\(k=0\\).**  \n  Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n\\).  All are non‑sunny,\n  so \\(k=0\\) is realised.\n\n* **\\(k=1\\).**  \n  Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) and one sunny line\n  through the single uncovered point \\((n,1)\\); for instance  \n\n  \\[\n  L:\\; y = x-n .\n  \\]\n\n  Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.2  The case \\(k\\ge 3\\)\n\nDefine, for \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k-1\\),\n\n\\[\nL_i:\\; y = \\frac{i-1}{k-i+1}\\,\\bigl(x-m\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nFor \\(i=k\\) we take the line through the two points\n\\((m+k,1)\\) and \\((m+1,2)\\); its equation is  \n\n\\[\nL_k:\\; y = \\frac{1}{k-1}\\,\\bigl(x-m\\bigr)+1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nAll slopes in (5) and (6) are different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\);\nhence every \\(L_i\\) is sunny.  The slopes are distinct, so the lines are\npairwise different.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n###### 2.2.1  Every point of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) lies on one of the \\(L_i\\)\n\nLet \\((x,y)=(m+i,j)\\in\\Delta_{k}\\) (so \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and\n\\(1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\)).  Put \\(t=j-1\\); then \\(0\\le t\\le k-i\\).\n\nChoose  \n\n\\[\nr = t+1\\qquad (1\\le r\\le k-i+1\\le k).\n\\]\n\nFor this index \\(r\\) the slope appearing in (5) (or (6) when \\(r=k\\))\nequals  \n\n\\[\ns_r=\\frac{r-1}{k-r+1}= \\frac{t}{k-t}.\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\(x=m+i\\) into the equation of \\(L_r\\) we obtain\n\n\\[\ny = s_r\\,(i)+1 = \\frac{t\\,i}{k-t}+1 .\n\\tag{8}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(t=j-1\\) and \\(k-t = k-j+1 = i\\) (the equality follows from\n\\(j\\le k+1-i\\)), the right–hand side of (8) simplifies to\n\n\\[\ny = \\frac{(j-1)i}{i}+1 = j .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((m+i,j)\\) satisfies the equation of \\(L_r\\); consequently every\npoint of \\(\\Delta_{k}\\) belongs to one of the lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 2.3  Final configuration\n\nFor a given admissible \\(k\\) we take\n\n* the \\(n-k\\) vertical lines (3), and  \n* the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(L_1,\\dots ,L_k\\) defined in §2.2.\n\nThe total number of lines is \\((n-k)+k=n\\); exactly \\(k\\) of them are\nsunny; and, by the discussion above, every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at\nleast one of the chosen lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\n* The impossibility of \\(k=2\\) was proved in §1.  \n* Constructions for all other non‑negative integers \\(k\\le n\\) were given\n  in §2.\n\nTherefore the set of all integers \\(k\\) for which the required family of\n\\(n\\) distinct lines exists is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1\\}\\cup\\{3,4,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
