            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n###**  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nS_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nIt is easy to count  \n\n\\[\n|S_{n}|=\\sum_{a=1}^{n}(n+1-a)=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}. \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nA line is **non‑sunny** if it is parallel to the \\(x\\)-axis \\((y=c)\\), to the \\(y\\)-axis \\((x=c)\\) or to the line \\(x+y=0\\) \\((x+y=c)\\).  \nA line is **sunny** if its slope is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 1.  The case \\(k=0\\) (all lines non‑sunny)\n\nConsider the following \\(n\\) distinct non‑sunny lines  \n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\nL_{i}&:x=i\\qquad &&(i=1,2,\\dots ,n-2),\\\\\nL_{n-1}&:y=1,\\\\\nL_{n}&:x+y=n+1 .\n\\end{aligned}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are non‑sunny: the first \\(n-2\\) are vertical, the \\((n-1)\\)-st is horizontal, the last one is parallel to \\(x+y=0\\).\n\nLet \\((a,b)\\in S_{n}\\).  \nIf \\(a\\le n-2\\) then \\((a,b)\\) lies on the vertical line \\(x=a\\) (one of the first \\(n-2\\) lines).  \nIf \\(a=n-1\\) then \\(b\\le 2\\) because \\(a+b\\le n+1\\); in both sub‑cases \\((a,b)\\) lies on the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nIf \\(a=n\\) then necessarily \\(b=1\\); this point lies on the diagonal line \\(x+y=n+1\\).  \n\nThus every point of \\(S_{n}\\) belongs to at least one line of the family (2).  \nConsequently a configuration with \\(k=0\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 2.  The case \\(k=1\\) (exactly one sunny line)\n\nAgain use the vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-2\\) and the horizontal line \\(y=1\\).  \nAll points of \\(S_{n}\\) with first coordinate at most \\(n-2\\) are already covered, and the horizontal line covers every point with second coordinate equal to \\(1\\).  \nThe only point of \\(S_{n}\\) that may remain uncovered is \\((n-1,2)\\) (indeed \\(n-1+2=n+1\\)).  \n\nDefine the line  \n\n\\[\nL:\\;y-1=\\frac{1}{\\,n-2\\,}\\bigl(x-1\\bigr) .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{n-2}\\); for \\(n\\ge3\\) this number is different from \\(0,\\infty,-1\\), hence \\(L\\) is sunny.  \nBecause \\((1,1)\\) satisfies (3) and the slope is \\(\\frac{1}{n-2}\\), the point \\((n-1,2)\\) also satisfies (3):\n\n\\[\n2-1=\\frac{1}{n-2}\\bigl((n-1)-1\\bigr)=\\frac{n-2}{n-2}=1 .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((n-1,2)\\in L\\).  \n\nAll other points of \\(S_{n}\\) are already covered by the vertical or horizontal lines, so the family consisting of the \\(n-2\\) vertical lines, the horizontal line \\(y=1\\) and the sunny line \\(L\\) covers \\(S_{n}\\).  \nExactly one line of the family is sunny, therefore a configuration with \\(k=1\\) exists for every \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 3.  The case \\(k=2\\) (exactly two sunny lines) – impossibility  \n\nAssume, for contradiction, that there is a set \\(\\mathcal L\\) of \\(n\\) distinct lines covering \\(S_{n}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.  \nLet \\(\\mathcal N\\) be the set of the \\(n-2\\) non‑sunny lines and let \\(U\\) and \\(V\\) be the two sunny lines.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.1  A counting observation  \n\nEach non‑sunny line is parallel to one of the three directions \\(x=\\text{const}\\), \\(y=\\text{const}\\) or \\(x+y=\\text{const}\\).  \nConsequently a non‑sunny line can contain at most one point of each “row’’ \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid a=\\text{const}\\}\\) and at most one point of each “column’’ \\(\\{(a,b)\\mid b=\\text{const}\\}\\).  \nSince the set \\(S_{n}\\) contains exactly \\(n\\) different rows (the values \\(a=1,\\dots ,n\\)) and also exactly \\(n\\) different columns (the values \\(b=1,\\dots ,n\\)), a non‑sunny line can contain at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\).  \n\nHence  \n\n\\[\n\\Bigl|\\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal N} (L\\cap S_{n})\\Bigr|\\le (n-2)n . \\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.2  Geometry of the two sunny lines  \n\nA sunny line is not parallel to any of the three forbidden directions; therefore each sunny line meets every row and every column in **exactly one** point of the plane.  \nConsequently each sunny line contains at most \\(n\\) points of \\(S_{n}\\) (one for each row, but possibly none for some rows because the corresponding point may lie outside the triangle \\(a+b\\le n+1\\)).  \n\nDenote by \\(A\\) the set of points of \\(S_{n}\\) that lie on at least one of the two sunny lines.  \nBecause the two sunny lines are distinct, they intersect in at most one point; therefore  \n\n\\[\n|A|\\le 2n-1 . \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n##### 3.3  A lower bound for the uncovered points  \n\nFrom (1) we have \\(|S_{n}|=\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}\\).  \nCombining (4) and (5) we obtain an upper bound for the total number of points that can be covered by the whole family \\(\\mathcal L\\):\n\n\\[\n\\bigl|( \\bigcup_{L\\in\\mathcal N}L )\\cup A\\bigr|\n\\le (n-2)n + (2n-1)=n^{2}-1 . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nBut for every \\(n\\ge3\\),\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-1 .\n\\]\n\nIndeed,\n\\[\nn^{2}-1-\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}= \\frac{2n^{2}-2-n^{2}-n}{2}= \\frac{n^{2}-n-2}{2}\n= \\frac{(n-2)(n+1)}{2}\\ge 0,\n\\]\nwith equality only when \\(n=2\\).  Since \\(n\\ge3\\), the right‑hand side is **strictly positive**, i.e.\n\n\\[\n\\frac{n(n+1)}{2}>n^{2}-1 .\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus the family \\(\\mathcal L\\) cannot cover all points of \\(S_{n}\\), contradicting the assumption.  \n\nTherefore a configuration with exactly two sunny lines does **not** exist for any \\(n\\ge3\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n#### 4.  Summary of the partial result  \n\n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) there exist configurations with \\(k=0\\) and with \\(k=1\\).  \n* For every integer \\(n\\ge3\\) no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists.  \n\nThe existence (or non‑existence) of configurations for the remaining values \\(k\\ge3\\) remains open.  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{The attainable values of }k\\text{ are }k=0\\text{ and }k=1;\\;k=2\\text{ is impossible.}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
