ust satisfy  \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{length}\\le 2+(n-2)-v=n-v .\n   \\]\n\n   Equality can hold only for the longest uncovered row, i.e. when \\(v=n-2\\).  \n   In that case the two uncovered rows are  \n\n   \\[\n   (n-1,1),\\;(n-1,2),\\;(n,1).\n   \\]\n\n   Any line that contains the point \\((n,1)\\) together with either \\((n-1,1)\\) (horizontal) or \\((n-1,2)\\) (slope \\(-1\\)) is **not** sunny.  \n   Hence the three points cannot be covered by only two sunny lines – a contradiction.  \n   Therefore **\\(k=2\\) is impossible for every \\(n\\ge 3\\).**\n\n4. **Construction for the remaining values of \\(k\\).**  \n\n   *\\(k=0\\).*  \n   Take the \\(n\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=n .\n   \\]\n\n   They are distinct, non‑sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n   *\\(k=1\\).*  \n   Use the \\(n-1\\) vertical lines \\(x=1,\\dots ,x=n-1\\) (non‑sunny).  \n   The only uncovered point is \\((n,1)\\); cover it with any sunny line, e.g.  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\tfrac12(x-n)+1 .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly one line is sunny.\n\n   *\\(k\\ge 3\\).*  \n   Write \\(v=n-k\\) (the number of vertical lines).  \n   Take the \\(v\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=v .\n   \\]\n\n   They cover completely the first \\(v\\) rows.  \n   The remaining rows are  \n\n   \\[\n   a=v+1,\\;v+2,\\dots ,n\n   \\]\n\n   and form a smaller triangle \\(T_k\\) of size \\(k\\) (its rows have lengths \\(k,k-1,\\dots ,1\\)).  \n   We now exhibit \\(k\\) sunny lines that cover **all** points of \\(T_k\\).  \n   The construction is by induction on \\(k\\).\n\n   *Base case \\(k=3\\).*  \n   The three sunny lines  \n\n   \\[\n   \\begin{aligned}\n   L_1 &: y = x-(v+1) \\qquad &\\text{through }(v+1,1)\\text{ and }(v+2,2),\\\\\n   L_2 &: y = -\\tfrac12x+\\tfrac{v+3}{2} \\qquad &\\text{through }(v+1,2)\\text{ and }(v+3,1),\\\\\n   L_3 &: y = -2x+2v+5 \\qquad &\\text{through }(v+1,3)\\text{ and }(v+2,1),\n   \\end{aligned}\n   \\]\n\n   are sunny (their